# Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored) for local development: `npm run dev` reads it, and
# nothing in it is ever deployed. It holds only what local development uses. A deployed
# environment's secrets (Access, and the email provider's keys) are stored with
# `npx wrangler secret put NAME --env production` instead; the Configuration reference
# lists them: https://getkestrel.dev/docs/configuration/#secrets
# Restart `npm run dev` after a change.

# --- Admin API auth ---
# Local dev authenticates with a JWT signed by DEV_AUTH_SECRET (the app verifies it
# via the same "verify token -> principal" contract Cloudflare Access uses in prod).
# The editor mints its own token from this on load — no other setup. It ships here
# rather than in wrangler.jsonc on purpose: a deployed env never gets this value, so
# the dev auth path stays fail-closed. In a deployed environment leave it unset; Access
# is the only gate.
DEV_AUTH_SECRET="dev-insecure-do-not-deploy"

# --- Minimum lead ---
# How long every send stays visible and cancelable before it fires
# (https://getkestrel.dev/docs/configuration/#min_lead_seconds).
# Deployed environments default to 300 (five minutes); local dev uses the one-minute floor
# that every environment shares, so a send scheduled a minute out behaves exactly as it
# would deployed. Not a secret: a deployed environment sets it in its `vars` in
# wrangler.jsonc. Below 60 or above 86400 (one day) is refused.
MIN_LEAD_SECONDS="60"

# --- Send simulation (local only) ---
# On a list send, local dev stands in for a real provider instead of accepting everything
# at once: the provider's batch size and pace, its failures, and delivery receipts that
# arrive after the send (delivered, bounced, complained), all through the real send loop.
# Test sends and confirmations go straight to the dev outbox (GET /api/dev/outbox),
# unsimulated. The profile names the provider modeled:
#   resend   Resend: 100 recipients a request, an idempotency key (a lost request is re-sent)
#   ses      Amazon SES: a second of its send rate a request (up to 50), one request a
#            second, no key (a lost request waits for Resolve), and its daily quota running
#            out once a send; a slice a minute on the default SUBREQUEST_BUDGET, as SES is
#            on Workers Free
#   1        a generic provider paced to be watched: small, slow batches
# Add ":none" (e.g. "ses:none") for a clean run with no failures, or set "off" for the
# plain instant fake. "not-connected" runs the app as a deployment with no email provider
# yet: every send refused, signups closed, a due send held. Switched back, the held send
# goes out at its next retry, a few minutes later.
# For one run, set it in the shell instead: SIMULATE_SENDS=ses npm run dev
# Only honored in a dev-shaped env (fake transport, no Access, APP_ORIGIN on localhost).
SIMULATE_SENDS="resend"
